  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM EDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight...Temperatures top out in the L-M90s this \nafternoon with peak heat indices continuing above 100F, but \nremaining below Heat Advisory criteria. Residents and visitors are\nstrongly encouraged to practice heat safety to avoid developing \nheat illness. Weak mid-level troughing continues across ECFL and \nwill enhance ISOLD-SCT (20-50%) diurnal shower/lightning storms \nthis afternoon and evening. Highest chances across the I-4\ncorridor where greatest moisture and strongest late day boundary \ncollisions occur. Storm motion is weak/erratic. Drier air aloft \nmay support local strong wind gusts (45-55 mph) in a few storms \nwith other storm impacts remaining frequent lightning strikes and \ntorrential downpours leading to quick 1-3\" rainfall totals for \nsome. Minor/nuisance flooding possible locally. Activity will wind\ndown through mid evening. Min temperatures fall into the M70s \nwith conditions humid. \n            \nTue-Tue Night...Surface ridge axis initially across the south-\ncentral FL peninsula begins to slowly lift northward. Light morning \nSW winds will \"back\" onshore in the afternoon with sea breeze \nformation and march inland. ISOLD-SCT showers/lightning storms with \nthe ECSB near the coast - quicker movement inland south; similarly \nthe quicker-moving WCSB will promote initial convection across our\nwestern coverage warning area. A late day/evening sea breeze \ncollision across the interior may send a few storms back to the \ncoast, esp Cape northward. PoPs 30-60% - highest interior & I-4 \ncorridor. Activity will wind down thru mid evening. Primary storm \nimpacts remain frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds 45-55 mph \nlocally, torrential downpours possibly leading to minor/nuisance \nflooding. Storm motion toward the NE at 5-10 mph.\n\nHigh temps continue in the L-M90s. Peak heat indices 100 to 107. \nOvernight lows consistent in the M70s. Cloud-cover thins thru the \nnight with conditions muggy.\n\nPrevious Extended Forecast Discussion Below.\n\nWed-Fri...As high pressure nudges essentially overhead, the low-\nlevel sea breeze circulation will be the dominant force for storm \ndevelopment each day. Light offshore flow in the mid-levels will try \nto steer showers and storms back toward the coast in the evening \nhours. As moisture returns to normal, this portends fairly high \ncoverage of afternoon and early evening showers and storms for east \ncentral Florida (50-70%).\n\nSeasonably hot conditions continue, with highs in the low to mid\n90s and heat indices of 100 to 107.\n\nNext Weekend into Monday...Near-normal moisture remains in place \nwith high pressure strengthening nearby or just northeast of the \nlocal area. Scattered to numerous afternoon and evening storms \nshould continue, but the focus may shift over the interior as mid-\nlevel offshore flow weakens. Continued seasonable heat and humidity.",
